e('salting multiplies attacker work by the number of users; slow hashing multiplies it by the cost factor.','distinct salts prevent sharing the same candidate-hash computation across accounts. Total attack cost also depends on targeting, password distribution and stopping rules; it is not universally an exact user-count multiplier. No hardware benchmark is supplied for these illustrative rates.','Avoidexactlinearmultiplierpromise explicitgap.',[o])
e('**TOTP** (RFC 6238) derives a 6-digit code from a shared secret and the current 30-second window. It defeats password reuse, but a phishing proxy can ask the victim for the code and replay it within seconds.','**TOTP** (RFC 6238) derives an output from a shared secret and a time counter. Six digits and a 30-second step are common settings; the RFC recommends the 30-second default rather than fixing every deployment to it. TOTP adds a barrier beyond a reused password, but its output can be relayed to the genuine verifier within the acceptance window.','Parametersconfigurable acceptancewindownotexact30.',[t])
e(' browser tells device: origin=bank.com\n device signs (challenge + origin)\n server verifies with public key',' browser checks RP ID and origin\n device signs authenticator data\n   + hash of browser client data\n server verifies signature + context','Actualsignaturestructure.',[w])
e('The browser, not the user, supplies the **origin**. On `bank-login.evil.com`, the device has no key for that origin, and a signature relayed from it would name the wrong origin, so the server rejects it. The user can\'t be tricked into handing over something they never see. And the server only stores public keys, so a database breach leaks nothing reusable.','The browser checks the relying-party (RP) ID against the calling origin and includes the origin and challenge in client data. The authenticator signs authenticator data plus the client-data hash. The server must verify the signature, challenge, expected origin, RP ID hash and required flags. A phishing origin cannot simply use a bank RP credential through a conforming browser. This resists credential phishing, but does not prevent compromised clients, session theft or weak recovery. Stealing the stored public key does not yield the private key; a broader database compromise may still expose sessions or other secrets.','CorrectRPbinding andlimit guarantees.',[w])
e('- **Sessions**: after login, a session token *is* the identity.','- **Sessions**: after login, a bearer session token conveys access to an authenticated session.','Tokennotidentity.')
e('- SMS, TOTP and push can all be phished in real time; passkeys can\'t, because the browser binds signatures to the origin.','- SMS, TOTP and conventional push approvals are relayable; correctly implemented WebAuthn resists credential phishing through RP and origin binding. Recovery and session attacks remain relevant.','Avoidabsoluteimmunity.',[w,n])
c(2,'explanation','Correct. Four independent uniform draws from 7776 words give about 51.7 bits, rounded to 52; eight non-space printable ASCII characters give about 52.4 bits.','Alphabetprecision.',option=3)
c(3,'workedExample','With one unsalted scheme, a candidate digest can be compared against many users. Unique salts require separate candidate-hash computations for each salt and prevent reusable precomputed tables across those salts. This does not guarantee an exact million-fold total cost for a million users: attackers may target a subset, stop early or exploit common-password distributions.','Removeexactmultiplier.',[o])
c(4,'prompt','Assume an illustrative rate of 10^10 unsalted SHA-256 candidates per second. Roughly how long to exhaust all eight-character passwords from the 94 non-space printable ASCII characters (94^8 ≈ 6.1 × 10^15)?','Hypotheticalbenchmark andalphabet.')
c(4,'explanation','At the assumed rate and search space, the calculation gives about seven days. No general hardware claim or comparison with every 128-bit system is needed.','Remove broadfeasibilityclaim.',option=3)
c(7,'prompt','Assume two million distinct accounts, no prior failures, one breached email/password pair tried once per account, and attempts spread over 50,000 residential IPs. Which control directly fails to trigger from those individual account attempts?','Originaldistinctaccountunstated andhelpsleastranking.')
c(7,'workedExample','With one attempt per distinct account and no prior failures, a five-failure account threshold is not reached. An even distribution gives 2,000,000 / 50,000 = 40 attempts per IP; whether IP throttling triggers depends on the time window and threshold. MFA, current blocklists and aggregate monitoring can reduce risk, but do not guarantee every campaign is stopped.','FixdefinitionandIPthresholdinference.')
c(8,'explanation','Correct for fixed-type, fixed-length digests: compare_digest avoids content-dependent short-circuit comparison. The surrounding login can still have timing differences.','Scopecomparisontiming.',[h],2)
c(8,'explanation','Its purpose is to avoid content-dependent short-circuiting. Relative speed depends on implementation and input, so no universal speed ranking is implied.','Originalnofasterunsupported.',[h],3)
c(8,'workedExample','Use compare_digest for these fixed-length byte digests to avoid a comparison whose work depends on the matching prefix. Type or length differences may be observable, and hashing, database lookups or account existence can affect total response time. This is one component of timing-resistant handling, not a proof that timing reveals nothing.','Removeabsolute timingguarantee.',[h])
c(9,'text','The code can be relayed within its acceptance window; correctly implemented WebAuthn resists this credential-phishing path through RP/origin binding','Successconditionalothercontrols.',[w,t],1)
c(9,'explanation','Correct. A TOTP output is not bound to the login origin. WebAuthn uses RP ID checks and signed client context, verified by the relying party, to prevent using its credential from an unrelated phishing origin.','Actualsignedcontext.',[w,t],1)
c(9,'workedExample','A proxy can relay valid password and TOTP values before the verifier rejects the time counter or re-use; other risk controls may still stop the login. Correct WebAuthn verification binds the authentication to the expected RP and origin instead. WebAuthn is a phishing-resistant method, not the only possible one; correctly implemented client-authenticated TLS can also provide phishing resistance under NIST guidance.','NotallphishingresistanceequalsFIDO.',[w,t,n])
c(10,'explanation','Correct. Origin/RP-bound cryptographic authentication replaces the relayable conventional push approval. Passkeys can still show consent or local-unlock prompts; the difference is what the approval authorises.','Passkeysnotpromptsabsent.',[w],2)
c(10,'workedExample','Uber reported repeated approval requests preceding account compromise in 2022. Number matching and push throttling reduce blind approval risk, though social engineering and relay remain possible. WebAuthn replaces that mechanism with RP/origin-bound cryptographic authentication; it can still prompt for local verification.','RetainprimaryincidentbutremoveunsupportedITdetail.',[ub,w])
c(11,'text','No stronger against inbox takeover than its recovery path, which can enrol a new passkey','Notexactnumericstrength.',option=2)
c(11,'workedExample','An attacker controlling the inbox can use the stated recovery route without defeating WebAuthn. This creates a different route to access; it does not assign an exact numeric strength. Protect recovery with appropriate independent checks and notifications, and assess delays or recovery codes in the complete threat model.','Weakestpathconcept notexactmetric.')
